    "headline": "Who is local AI for? Matthew Berman argues it does not pay off for the average user today",
    "summary_short": "Commentator Matthew Berman thinks running AI on your own computer is currently expensive, hard and worse than the leading models. Even so, he argues it must work for the sake of privacy and independence.",
    "summary_long": "Berman distinguishes between local AI, which runs on your device even without internet, and open-weight models, which anyone can download. The best open models, often Chinese, are just one generation behind the leading ones. But they are so large they do not fit on a home computer: they need racks of GPUs costing hundreds of thousands of dollars.\n\nThat is why, he argues, asking someone to drop ChatGPT for local AI is kidding yourself. Even he, with plenty of compute and expertise, turns to ChatGPT or Claude when he needs results. He criticizes that the debate focuses on \"look what I've got running\" rather than on what gets done. In favor of local AI he cites control over your own data and not depending on a lab cutting off your access.",
